## One Interface, Many Types

When multiple types satisfy the same interface, a single generic function works with all of them. Each type brings its own behavior.

### Three Types, One Interface

```xiom
interface Greetable {
  fn greet() -> Str;
}

type Person = {
  name: Str;
}

fn Person.greet(self) -> Str {
  return "Hi, I'm " + self.name;
}

type Robot = {
  model: Str;
}

fn Robot.greet(self) -> Str {
  return "Beep boop, model " + self.model;
}

type Pet = {
  pet_name: Str;
  species: Str;
}

fn Pet.greet(self) -> Str {
  return self.pet_name + " the " + self.species + " wags tail";
}
```

Now all three satisfy `Greetable` — each has a `greet() -> Str` method. No special keyword needed.

### One Function Rules Them All

```xiom
fn announce[T: Greetable](thing: &T) {
  io.println(thing.greet());
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let person = Person{ name: "Alex" };
  let robot = Robot{ model: "RX-9000" };
  let pet = Pet{ pet_name: "Fluffy", species: "cat" };

  announce(&person);  // "Hi, I'm Alex"
  announce(&robot);   // "Beep boop, model RX-9000"
  announce(&pet);     // "Fluffy the cat wags tail"
  return 0;
}
```

Same function `announce`, three completely different behaviors — polymorphism in action.

### The Key Insight

The function `announce` only knows that T satisfies `Greetable`. It doesn't know if T is a Person, Robot, or Pet. It only calls `.greet()`. This is **abstraction** — hiding details behind a shared interface. The compiler checks at the CALL SITE that whatever type you pass actually has a `greet()` method.

